  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B014",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B018",
    "B032"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. Optical lattice clocks unite a discrete physical invention—the magic-wavelength solution to trapping many atoms without compromising clock accuracy—with defining experimental realization and unusually strong maturity. They are reproducible precision instruments, with systematic effects controlled across laboratories and demonstrated reach to timekeeping, relativistic geodesy, and fundamental-physics measurements. Katori and Ye remain the most compact attribution for the central concept and its decisive development, provided the award is explicitly confined to optical lattice clocks rather than the entire optical-clock enterprise.",
    "case_against": "The attribution boundary remains the essential reservation: broader optical-frequency metrology and subsequent lattice-clock development involve many important contributors. The citation must therefore not overclaim. Circuit QED remains a scientifically exceptional alternative in my own field, but the unresolved boundary with recently recognized superconducting-circuit advances and the plausible indispensability of additional theorists prevent a sufficiently clean three-person allocation at present.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that the magic-wavelength lattice-clock idea solved the problem of using many trapped atoms without degrading clock accuracy, and that its systematic control and independent realization make it a mature measurement platform.",
        "response": "I agree. This is the decisive comparison in favor of B014: its value is not simply extreme performance, but a controllable physical regime that has persisted beyond an initial demonstration. I also share Kroll's caution that the historical record must distinguish this achievement from the wider optical-clock revolution."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig favors B032 because Jarzynski, Crooks, and Seifert established a general framework for irreversibility, while acknowledging uncertainty about its experimental prominence and the boundary among related theorems.",
        "response": "The conceptual generality of B032 is a serious virtue, and I retain it as an alternative. But its prize unit combines several related relations and a later framework, whereas B014 has a more singular physical solution and a more direct chain from concept to independently reproducible experimental capability. On present evidence, that clearer bounded achievement takes precedence."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson supports B014 while judging neodymium-iron-boron magnets a powerful alternative whose original attribution must be separated from prior materials work and later optimization.",
        "response": "I find that comparison persuasive. B022 has unquestioned practical consequence, but the required historical separation is material. B014 similarly requires careful attribution work, yet its central magic-wavelength principle and defining realization yield a more focused discovery-to-demonstration chain."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Historical review should confirm that Katori and Ye adequately delimit the foundational optical-lattice-clock achievement, while identifying the precise boundary from broader optical-clock work.",
      "The citation should be tested for whether \"invention and development\" is sufficiently narrow, or whether explicit reference to the magic-wavelength lattice-clock principle better preserves the intended scope.",
      "For B007, a fuller attribution analysis is still needed to determine whether the achievement can be separated from neighboring superconducting-circuit breakthroughs within the three-laureate limit."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No specific conflict requiring recusal is apparent from the supplied profile. I work in superconducting quantum physics and lead a programme connected to that wider research community, so I have applied particular caution to B007; no identified collaboration or institutional connection with its proposed laureates is supplied."
  }
}
